WEBVTT

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:05.440
Transponders are a broadcasting device required

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.320
to be active on all ships in order to notify

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.610
others of their presence.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.030
A ship's name, registry, present location, and heading...

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.620
The transponder's information is human will and goals.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.540
When sailors understand one another's ships,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.860
the quietness of space becomes a golden sea.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.920
In this era, space is still filled

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.420
with memories of the great age of exploration.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.380
I'd like to change the flight plan a little.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.010
It's going to be a long night.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.740
Look at this.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.280
Our original plan was to have the Odette II

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.330
go around the far side of Sand of
the Red Star, away from Tau Ceti.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:30.580
So I thought it might be faster if we start our turn sooner

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.590
and go inside Sand of the Red Star's orbit.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.380
It'll shave some time off the flight plan,

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.390
and get us back to the relay station a little faster.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.390
And?

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.430
It isn't only to save time.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.930
The reason you changed our path...

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:49.770
Explain that as well.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:56.440
Well, the biggest reason is that
we can limit the enemy's actions.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.080
We're almost inside the Sand of the Red Star's gravity field.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.790
The area around the planet
is within radar range.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:14.460
But we haven't seen any ships
that seem likely to attack us.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.340
If there is an enemy after us,

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:20.380
there's only one possible reason they haven't shown themselves.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.970
They're hiding somewhere.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.590
And where is that somewhere?

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:28.890
There are currently only two areas that the Odette II's radar can't see.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:33.860
The far side of Sand of the Red Star, or inside the sun,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.860
where the radiation is too strong
for radar to function properly.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:39.860
I vote for the one that isn't the sun.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.490
Agreed.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.610
One vote for "far side of the planet."

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:48.200
One of the basic rules of air combat
is to always be closer to the sun.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.580
That's a good idea.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.620
I don't see any spaceships there likely to cause trouble.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.250
And it's within the error margin
for our submitted flight plan.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:00.760
This change to the flight plan means that

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.300
we won't be closest to Sand of the Red Star on the morning of the fourth day.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.900
We will be closest at midnight, on the third day.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.600
So the battle will begin
while the teachers are asleep?

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.480
Think it'll work that way?

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.650
It's possible that they won't attack until morning.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:19.110
That would allow us to gain some more distance from Sand of the Red Star.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.450
And the extra sunlight would mean
we'd gain that much speed.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.580
And in the worst case, if nothing happens, I'll say...

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.660
"I'm sorry! It was all in my mind."

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.580
Then that's just fine.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.920
Okay, everyone, it'll be tonight.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:30.810
Has it started?

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:31.600
Not yet.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.020
The most dangerous times were right after our check-in,

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.480
or when we're closest to Sand of the Red Star, right?

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:37.940
Yeah.

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:43.730
<b>Closest Point to "Sand of the Red Star"</b>

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:47.410
So I think it'll begin once we've sent our check-in.
<b>Closest Point to "Sand of the Red Star"</b>

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:49.870
Sending our check-in...

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:56.040
I'll update our position and say there are no problems, okay?

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.000
Maybe we should add that

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.460
we're preparing to engage an unknown enemy warship!

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.670
No.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:03.630
Do it as usual.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.460
I'm sorry.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:12.050
If everything goes well, we'll be able to send the same message an hour from now.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.270
Hi.

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:16.730
No one saw you?

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:18.850
I made sure that no one was in the hallways.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:23.190
All that's left is for you to intercept the internal ship monitor,

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.400
and no one will have seen the pretty doctor sneaking

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.740
into the captain's room at night.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.490
Thanks.

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.990
I'm supposed to be married, after all.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:37.290
So, everyone's on the bridge after lights out?

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:38.410
Is this okay?

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.250
I took some minimal precautions.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:44.960
I turned off the monitors for the infirmary, your room, and mine.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:51.090
All that's left is to pretend we were asleep
should anyone call us, in case of emergency.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.050
Think they will?

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.810
If they need to.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.220
Miss Marika makes quick assessments.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:01.940
She'll call us before things get bad enough that our help is needed.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.690
It seems like you have a lot of faith in her.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:08.530
You saw her yacht flight simulator.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.490
How quickly she makes decisions, and her ability to stick to them.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:13.950
So what's the situation?

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.580
They just sent

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:19.330
their standard check-in, via FTL communication.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.750
And we've almost passed the closest distance to Sand of the Red Star.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.710
If the Marika is right,

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:29.670
the enemy will soon initiate its cyber-warfare attack.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:32.720
Is that it?

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:35.220
It's here.

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.810
It really came.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.350
The whole effective radius is flooded with noise.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.520
Wow.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.690
So this is real electronic jamming?

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:47.780
It's the exact same reading I saw
in the textbook from that military site.

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:50.150
Of course, just flooding the area with jamming waves

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.530
is the simplest trick in the book.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:53.490
It's nothing special.

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:58.040
We're all new to this,
so that works to our advantage.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.330
Honestly, even if they knock our eyes out now,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.960
it doesn't really matter.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:08.500
Even flying blind, there isn't anything out here to hit.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.130
And even without radar,

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:13.050
we can use the direction of sunlight and gravity

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:14.970
and fly with just our instruments.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:16.010
What do we do?

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:19.310
Send an FTL message to flight control with the situation?

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:20.770
Not yet.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.230
The enemy wants us to open an FTL line.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.650
If they can hijack a zero time-lag FTL line,

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.360
we won't be able to do anything at all.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.070
But with our electronic warfare systems,

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.410
it won't be easy for them, will it?

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:38.780
Well, that's true.

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:41.160
And there it is.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:42.910
I've got hacking on our normal line.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:46.500
If they're smart,
they'll hit radar and comms at the same time.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.000
Everything is by the book.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.670
That just means they'll be easier to fight.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.380
It's easier to deal with this way.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:52.880
Then?

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.720
W-Well, for now, we follow the plan.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.340
Act like we think it's an error.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:01.520
Try pretending that you're fiddling with the radar screen settings,

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.560
and adjusting the frequency.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.600
Do it slowly,

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.560
as though only two people were on duty.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.110
Don't work too fast.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.280
I couldn't do it fast anyway.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:15.200
Don't worry.

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:18.030
System check.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:21.410
I've confirmed the radar is fine as far as the antenna connection.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:23.500
Next, maybe I'll reset the switches.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:24.620
Thanks.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:26.210
How about the comm line?

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.710
They've found their way into
the isolated dummy system.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.130
As we planned, they believe it's the Odette II,

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.840
and are doing their best to break it.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:35.880
I'm fighting back as hard as I can,

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.010
but the default security settings are pretty easy to break.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.970
Yeah. It was easy this time because we took precautions,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.560
but if we hadn't noticed, before we knew it...

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:47.770
If we'd lost control to the outside,

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:49.980
they'd be able to control not only our heading,

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.730
but our life support systems.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:55.820
If we'd set the electronic countermeasures to automatic,

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:57.240
would it have worked?

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:58.570
Probably not.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:03.790
If these are the same guys you fought at the relay station,

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.410
they've seen our automatic systems once.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.460
The same pattern won't work twice.

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.960
I think they'd have prepared something to deal with it,

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:15.800
and we would have been taken over even faster.

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:16.670
Then...

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.430
How did they conduct electronic warfare in the past?

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.100
I think the same way they do now.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.010
In the end, it's all human power.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:29.100
You can have computers break through security or match quantum codes,

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.230
but you still have to be the one to decide where to start,

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:34.150
or what to use.

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:38.360
If it fails, you have to be the one to tell it what to do next.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.570
And you have to manually repel attacks that

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.870
the automatic systems can't deal with.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.700
The judgment of the person on duty is everything.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:47.370
It's surprisingly primitive.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.380
Dummy systems have been 50% hijacked.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.630
I'd like to reboot the radar,

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:59.510
but if they're still jamming, it won't help.

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:01.550
What do we do?

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.470
We don't know when they'll quit jamming us.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:08.560
In this case, I'm more concerned about
not having radar, even for a second.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.150
Then just make the radar stop emitting.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.980
If we do, they'll think we believe it's broken,

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.610
and are rebooting it.

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.570
If we just shut down the emitter,
we can quickly bring it back.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:21.610
That's a good idea.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.490
Shut down the radar emitter.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:24.410
Right.

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:25.950
Shutting down the emitter.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.660
Any other preparations?

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:29.620
Stop the ship.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:30.330
And?

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:33.080
Are all of the Odette II's systems functioning normally?

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:34.540
Navigation systems are fine.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:36.630
Primary and secondary engines are on line.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.300
Output is steadily increasing.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.260
Sail systems are all green.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.340
Masts are functioning normally.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.050
Sails are as well.

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:44.800
Normal comm systems are still being hacked.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:46.890
FTL comm systems are on standby.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:49.350
Of course, the electronic warfare systems are ready.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.100
Oh, I forgot!

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.770
Seal all bulkheads.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:54.100
You have do that too.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.270
That's right.

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:57.020
We don't know what'll happen.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.030
So let's do what we can.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:00.570
Seal all bulkheads.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.500
Dummy system 100% under enemy control.

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.580
Outside communications have ceased.

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:18.050
Everyone, are you ready?

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:24.010
Begin combat operations.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:33.350
Begin electronic warfare.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:37.440
The enemy should think we're
under their control and reveal themselves.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.110
Restore the radar before they confirm.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:42.110
Radar emitter has been rebooted.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:46.240
There it is.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:49.700
A ship is behind us, near Sand of the Red Star.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:51.910
Their transponder is on.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.040
They think they have us.

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:56.170
The Lightning 11, registered in West Kilia.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:57.040
I knew it.

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.340
They've stopped their electronic warfare attack

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:04.050
which means they think they're in control and have let their guard down.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:05.380
That's good.

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.350
Emergency message from the Lightning 11.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.010
There they are.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:13.020
"From: Lightning 11.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:15.980
To: Captain of the Odette II.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:19.360
Your ship is now under our control.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:22.150
Surrender immediately."

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.610
It's an official message, and they aren't naming themselves.

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.110
They really are underestimating us.

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:29.830
Don't respond yet.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:33.370
It hasn't been that long since they jammed our radar.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:39.540
It's night on our ship, so it'd be unnatural
for the captain to respond too quickly.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:40.750
That's right.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:44.840
We're supposed to have been caught unaware, right?

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:47.300
What are they doing?

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.720
They're confirming our ship is under their control.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:53.220
And they're looking through the internal monitors.

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:54.930
They're peeking?

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:56.060
Oh, man.

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:56.640
It's okay.

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:01.650
When I made the dummy system,
I loaded it with images from our library.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:06.150
All they'll see are empty rooms
and two bored people on night watch.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.620
They're trying to ring our alarms.

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:11.330
What are they thinking?

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:14.830
They're kindly trying to alert a bunch of idiot high school girls,

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:16.910
who didn't even realize their

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.880
ship was taken over, to an emergency.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:21.090
And there it goes.

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:27.090
Which means all the idiot high school girls have been woken up.

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.640
As the idiot president of the yacht club,

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:32.810
maybe I should send a half-awake answer.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:35.520
Route setting is complete.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:40.600
I've used their FTL line to link directly to their core systems.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:44.780
They attacked with electronic warfare,
but their security's a piece of crap.

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:46.150
There you go, president.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:47.400
Your answer?

00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:48.110
Roger.

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:51.740
"From: Captain of the Odette II."

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:53.080
No...

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:56.700
"President of the Hakuoh Girls' Academy Yacht Club, Jenny Dolittle."

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.420
"To: Captain of the Lightning 11.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:01.330
Body: Fools!"

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:02.170
That is all.

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:05.550
"Subject: Fools!"

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:07.710
Want me to say it again?

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:09.550
N-No...

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.180
O-Okay, vice-president.

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:17.350
As soon as she transmits her message,
start your electronic attack.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:18.140
Roger!

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:19.850
Okay, here we go.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:21.060
Transmitted.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:23.940
Use the radar to jam their communications!

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:25.400
Roger.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.530
"Spring has come and gone, and summer is here.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:29.990
They say in summer, we hang our white robes on Heavenly Kagu Mountain."

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:31.410
They've responded with another poem.

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:34.240
"You swore to love me forever,
but I don't know if I can believe you.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.490
We parted this morning,
my heart disordered, as I pondered."

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.450
I guess they know what they're doing.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:39.040
The enemy's shut down the line.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:42.120
But we have lots of good equipment here.

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:43.830
You won't get away!

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:46.500
There it is!

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:47.920
The enemy's jamming waves!

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:48.880
But it's too late.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:50.550
And weak.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:53.760
All we have to do is up our output...

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:57.260
"The Odette II pushes forward, through the blue,

00:15:57.260 --> 00:15:59.390
like the scent of a blooming flower."

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.350
In terms of sheer output,
we have as much as a battleship.

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:07.110
Okay... They're ours.

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:09.030
Knock out their engines.

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:09.570
"Mountain cherry tree, love me as I love you.

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.490
Leave just enough power for them to communicate.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:11.780
Because I'm alone in the depths of the mountain,

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:12.820
Roger.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:13.490
I've emergency shut down their primary engines.

00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:14.780
and there's no one here but you."

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:15.780
I've emergency shut down their primary engines.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.990
And now, their backups, just in case...

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:24.290
What's wrong?

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:27.790
There's nothing there?

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:30.710
What's wrong? A system problem?

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:35.090
No, the resistance I encountered when I was messing with their systems

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:36.550
has suddenly disappeared.

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:40.850
The communication line to the Lightning 11

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:42.020
has been shut down.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:44.770
No...

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:46.900
That isn't all they shut down.

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:50.400
They powered off their computers.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:52.110
What does that mean?

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:56.570
They turned off their computer,
so our victory is meaningless.

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:57.280
Meaningless?

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:01.620
Energy wave approaching.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:14.420
What do you mean by "energy wave"?

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.390
I think they're aiming at us with a beam cannon.

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:18.180
What? They shot at us? For real?

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:19.470
Isn't their computer down?

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:21.010
They weren't just trying to scare us?

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:21.220
As long as their primary power's on line,

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:22.430
They're just firing blind!

00:17:22.430 --> 00:17:23.180
What if it hits? What do we do?
As long as their primary power's on line,

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:24.730
they can do whatever they want.

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:27.690
That's why they shut it down before I could cut it off?

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:31.570
Another one.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.990
No radar waves, and no response on the comm line.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:41.660
How are they aiming at us?

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:45.330
Any decent gunport will have optical targeting systems.

00:17:45.870 --> 00:17:47.040
Optical targeting systems?

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:52.920
What?

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.630
How do you use long-range ballistics without any radar?

00:17:55.630 --> 00:18:01.470
Well, you use your eye to look through
the targeting scope, and you adjust the gauge.

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:03.640
By eye?

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:05.020
Another one.

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:05.850
It's closer than before.

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:09.270
Is it luck?

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:11.650
Or are they adjusting each time they fire?

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:12.860
What do we do?

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:14.400
Do we just let them shoot us?

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:17.200
This ship has no beam cannons and no shields!

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:20.320
It's fine.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:24.160
The Lightning 11 is firing from range. It can't move.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:26.540
If we switch to normal propulsion
and accelerate, we can run—

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:27.160
Don't!

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:30.170
If we fire our engines now,

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:31.920
they'll be able to use infrared sensors,

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:33.050
not just optical targeting!

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:34.920
They'll know our exact location!

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:36.260
But we can't return fire!

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:37.220
We can!

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.970
Calculate their position as accurately as you can.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:42.100
What are you going to do?

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:42.800
Use a secret weapon!

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:45.100
Excuse me.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:46.100
R-Right...

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:46.980
Let's see...

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:52.060
It's getting closer!

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:53.440
High-frequency radar,

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.990
generate an accurate positioning for the Lightning 11.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:00.200
Like this?

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:01.370
Okay.

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:05.790
The enemy is 400,000 kilometers behind us,
with a total width of 30 meters.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:07.660
This one, too...

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:11.380
Go!

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:14.670
What?

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:15.550
Don't worry.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:18.630
The masts are moving to match my adjustments.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:21.890
What did you do?

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:26.310
If you set their permeability to zero,
the solar sails will reflect 100% of the sunlight.

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:30.390
And if we adjust them so they all point at a single spot behind us...

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.320
Any more beams?

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:46.200
None.

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:47.990
What is the Lightning 11 doing?

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:49.160
Nothing.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.580
Its engines are still off line, and there's no signal.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:54.000
Huh, their infrared's going up.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:55.170
Did we get them?

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:57.050
Knocked them out with the sunlight?

00:20:00.630 --> 00:20:02.380
We don't need to burn them up.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:04.680
We just have to keep them from using their optical targeting systems.

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:05.390
It moved.

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:07.430
Which way?

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:09.890
Is it following us?  Or...

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:11.270
Wait... No, there are more?

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:12.230
More?

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:15.860
One... No, two.

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:21.490
The reinforcements are here.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:22.950
But both of them...

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:24.570
Their transponders are on.

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:27.240
The one to the north is

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.040
the pirate ship Bentenmaru.

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:31.250
The other is

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:33.870
the pirate ship Barbalusa?

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:35.830
That's my ship.

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:41.260
I'm glad I came to see who might be the next captain.

00:20:42.590 --> 00:20:44.930
Allow me to introduce myself again.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:50.060
I am the daughter of the space pirate ship Barbalusa's captain, Kenjo Kurihara.

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:51.390
My name is Chiaki Kurihara.

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:57.150
Wow, they're not holding back...

00:20:57.150 --> 00:20:59.320
Neither our ship nor the Barbalusa.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:01.690
The Stellar Military is here too.

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:06.660
This is the Stellar Military's Sixth Escort Fleet.

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:09.530
Odette II, we have you on radar.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:12.040
Is there anything you need?

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:13.830
This is the Odette II.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:15.500
We're fine for now.

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:16.460
Thank you.

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.040
Good sailing.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.850
The Hakuchou, one of the Original Seven...

00:21:30.850 --> 00:21:32.810
I think it still has a lot of fight in it.

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:44.110
Mom.

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.360
I'll do it.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:48.530
I'll be captain.

00:21:49.910 --> 00:21:53.250
I'll be a space pirate.

00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:02.250
Live long and prosper.

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:03.920
Right.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.300
C.M.S. C.M.A. O.P.R. Into the space

00:22:08.300 --> 00:22:11.510
Marika Makes a Decision

00:22:11.510 --> 00:22:12.510
C.M.S. C.M.A. O.P.R. Into the space

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:18.020
M.C.Z. Overall O.I.E. What's goin' on?

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:21.020
Welcome glory, let's breakthrough and fly out now, let's go!

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:24.400
Run towards an unlimited dimension. Come, let's accelerate beyond this

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.490
history that's to be known, regardless of time. Fly towards a destined future.

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:30.530
Just a fossil if you give up a miracle. Your decision is your best choice.

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:39.460
There's another, unknown, me hiding in my heart.

00:22:39.460 --> 00:22:44.340
M.C.Z. Overall O.I.E. What's goin' on?

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:48.170
How far?

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:50.840
Go, go, go, go, ring a bell! Go, go, ring, ring a bell!

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:54.470
Until the end.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:55.810
Go, go, go, go, ring a bell!

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:59.140
You're lost and missing.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:07.990
We're lost without a hope of any shelter.

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:11.740
But why? What did we do? What did we do?

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.160
What should we do?

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:16.330
From the world beyond shadows invade.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:20.620
We're destined to stop them with our lives.

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:26.670
So no matter where we go, as far as it takes

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:30.930
we'll always believe in ourselves.

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:41.390
The girl has made her decision.

00:23:41.390 --> 00:23:45.570
What was supposed to be a fun summer vacation has become difficult training.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:46.780
The expectations of the people around her...

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:47.940
Her own nervousness...

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:52.570
Amidst all this, her first voyage as a pirate begins.

00:23:54.120 --> 00:23:55.910
Next time, on <i>Bodacious Space Pirates:</i>

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:57.700
Marika's first day at work.

00:23:57.700 --> 00:23:59.040
Look forward to it.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:05.000
Marika's First Day at Work

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:10.130
Begin Combat Operations.
